  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA trough moving onto the West Coast has displaced the high pressure \ndome eastward. This has allowed flow aloft over the Desert \nSouthwest and Southern Great Basin to shift to more southerly- \nsouthwesterly, which is advecting in monsoonal moisture. PWATs on \nthis evening's 00z KGJT sounding are roughly the same as at 00z \nyesterday, however the profile is saturated to a lower level in the \natmosphere. This increased level of saturation was evident in more \nprecipitation reaching the ground today, with wetting rains observed \nacross and south of the San Juans. By Wednesday, saturation down to \nthe surface is expected, with widespread showers and thunderstorms \nin the afternoon, and wetting rain across the higher elevations. \nThis will lead to an increased threat for flash flooding and burn \nscar debris flows as we move into Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nPer ensemble forecasts, moisture, and therefore shower and storm \ncoverage, is expected to peak on Thursday, with both Wednesday and \nFriday seeing widespread showers and storms as well. This means that \nThursday has the highest threat for heavy rains, flash flooding, and \ndebris flows. However, there comes the question of morning cloud \ncover. Hi-Res guidance is favoring a very monsoonal pattern of \novernight convection the next several nights, with cloud cover \nlingering into the morning hours. Should this cloud cover be \nparticularly dense and long-lasting, it will impact how much \ninstability can develop over eastern Utah and western Colorado, and \ntherefore how much precipitation will be convective versus \nstratiform. This will also impact timing of storm development, with \nlingering cloud cover displacing initiation later into the \nafternoon. This will be something to keep an eye on, especially when \nmoisture is maximized on Thursday. The other question is that of a \nsource of lift. Terrain is a big one, with afternoon showers and \nstorms expected to form first over the ridges. However, there are \nalso subtle waves rotating around the edge of the high that could \ngive some extra lift over the next several days, as well as \nboundaries left over from the previous day's convection. There is \nless certainty in the impact from the shortwaves and the lingering \nboundaries, which does throw some uncertainty over where convection \nwill develop once it's moved off the ridges. So while details are \nstill a bit murky, confidence is high that we're moving into a \nwetter period for the second half of this work week, and even into \nthe weekend.\n\nTemperatures will cool through the back half of the week as showers, \nstorms, and cloud cover rule the day. Near-normal highs on Wednesday \nwill cool to close to ten degrees below normal by Friday. Overnight \nlows will also cool to near-normal levels, which will be a welcome \nrelief.",
